WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.380
Strap a harness onto a breeding skylark.

2
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:11.100
It adds three to four grams, about a tenth of the bird's body mass.

3
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.440
Its nest tends to produce fewer fledglings.

4
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.040
Yet nobody took any food away.

5
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.080
So where did the chicks go?

6
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:22.793
Every organism works from a limited budget.

7
00:00:23.753 --> 00:00:29.473
Energy and nutrients are split among growth, maintenance and repair, storage, and

8
00:00:29.473 --> 00:00:35.173
reproduction. Like a monthly paycheck, more for one means less for another.

9
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:38.313
That negative link is a trade-off.

10
00:00:39.053 --> 00:00:44.953
So a cost paid out of a fixed budget may leave less for young, for upkeep, or for

11
00:00:44.953 --> 00:00:45.953
both.

12
00:00:46.017 --> 00:00:52.557
Now the puzzle. In many bird populations, birds with big clutches survive better.

13
00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:55.097
That does not refute a trade-off.

14
00:00:55.737 --> 00:00:58.137
Some individuals acquire more than others.

15
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:02.837
A bird that gathers more can afford more eggs and a longer life.

16
00:01:03.457 --> 00:01:05.897
So across birds, the link is positive.

17
00:01:06.657 --> 00:01:11.477
For any one bird, though, more spent on eggs still leaves less for itself.

18
00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:16.297
To see the trade-off, change one thing on purpose.

19
00:01:17.237 --> 00:01:19.677
Enlarge a brood. Add a handicap.

20
00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:21.797
Add back one nutrient.

21
00:01:22.877 --> 00:01:26.057
Now take a meta-analysis of 30 bird species.

22
00:01:27.337 --> 00:01:31.097
Naturally larger clutches went with better parental survival.

23
00:01:32.197 --> 00:01:36.177
Enlarged broods lowered survival on average, with a small effect.

24
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:40.217
But the interval for each added chick includes zero.

25
00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:44.057
So a fixed cost per extra egg is not supported.

26
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.170
Now the skylark numbers, from first broods.

27
00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.330
19 of 25 control nests succeeded.

28
00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.850
8 of 17 handicapped nests did.

29
00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.230
That is 76% against 47.1%.

30
00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.650
The gap is 28.9 percentage points.

31
00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.630
Measured against the control rate, it is a 38.1% reduction.

32
00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.810
Those are two different numbers.

33
00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.230
Percentage points compare two rates directly.

34
00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.170
Percent compares one rate to the other.

35
00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:23.370
Mean fledglings per nest fell from 2.0 to 1.2.

36
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.680
So the harness and load together reduced reproductive output in this population.

37
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.760
The nest success gap was borderline.

38
00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.140
The study did not establish a survival cost to the adults.

39
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:43.100
And no group wore a harness alone, so harness and load cannot be separated.

40
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:50.300
Keep this rule. A natural correlation can hide a trade-off, so change one thing on

41
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.140
purpose and compare with controls.
